From 44971ead808bd3784641312538ba1023ce0deb7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shaurizard <contact@nyoom.engineering>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:35:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 20/32] input: read SDL pads on a timer thread instead of the
 guest's XInput calls

Every XInputGetState, GetCapabilities, SetState and GetKeystroke call of
the guest queued SDL_PumpEvents on the UI thread, and an event watch
copied every joystick and gamepad event (one per axis of every HID
report) into a queue that the next call replayed under the driver's
lock. Rumble was sent from the guest thread, under that lock. Under
Wine each HID read and rumble report is several wineserver and
winedevice round trips, so the UI thread and the guest thread that polls
the pad both paid for device I/O.

Read the pads on an SDL timer every 4 ms instead (SDL_UpdateJoysticks,
then each pad's buttons and axes through its mapping), and keep the
result; the guest's calls copy it under the lock and never call into
SDL. SDL_HINT_AUTO_UPDATE_JOYSTICKS is off, since the timer updates the
pads, and the per-sample joystick, gamepad and sensor events are
disabled, so SDL no longer builds and dispatches an event per report.
An event filter takes only the device add and remove events, which the
timer handles: it alone opens, closes, reads and rumbles the pads, and
it sends the latest motor speeds outside the lock. Capabilities are
read on the timer too, so the wireless flag still follows power
information that arrives after the pad connects.

The joystick-added log, the XInput subtype and the hotplug logging from
development are kept; the subtype, name and GUID are read when the pad
is opened, so EnumerateDevices needs no SDL call either.

Measured on the Steam Deck under Proton, against the previous build: the
UI thread took 1.4 % of a core (9.0 %), the guest thread that polls the
pad 14.4 % (19.9 %) and the timer 5.3 %; a later race ran at 80.8 fps,
1 % low 49.7 (80.0, 51.2).

